^^Introduction to the partitive case^^

When a person talks about their journey learning Finnish, they are bound to mention one specific thing as the hardest to grasp and learn: the partitive case. Because of this fact, a lot of thought and planning has gone into designing this particular portion of the Finnish course, so that you may get a solid introduction to the concept as well as learn a great deal of it. Rather than having this be inside a particular skill or unit, because this concept really needs to be learned in context, it extends through the rest of the course and comes up with regular intervals and with new examples. This first part will introduce it as simply as possible.


^What is partitive?^

Partitive, as the name implies, denotes 'parts' or 'without result or specific amount'. Most simply, it is an unspecified amount of something, as opposed to the whole thing, and can be used for nouns, pronouns and adjectives. The partitive ending is usually -a/-ta (-ä/-tä), like you have learned in Unit 0 with the phrase *juon vettä* (I drink water) instead of *juon veden* (I drink the water).

Short bullet list:
- use with uncountable nouns
- endings mostly -a/-ta (-ä/-tä) *kylmä* --> *kylmää*, *vesi* --> *vettä*
- a word ending in -nen --> -sta/-stä: *ihminen --> ihmistä*
- verbs like *rakastaa*, *vihata*, *odottaa* always require partitive: *Odotan ihmistä.* (I am waiting for the person.)
- if a noun gets partitive, all adjectives/pronouns that describe it also get partitive: *kaksi isoa nopeaa kaunista kissaa* (two big quick beautiful cats)
- 'sinä' in partitive is *sinua* and 'hän' is *häntä*: *Rakastan sinua.* (I love you.)